# REGRESSION — an implicit-`it` body passed to a COLLECTION higher-order
# method is a closure BODY, not a callable value.
#
# `extract_hof_closure` pulls the parameter names and result expression out of
# a HOF argument. For an argument that is not a closure LITERAL it synthesizes
# a forwarding wrapper `(p0): arg(p0)` — correct for `xs.map(double)`, where
# `arg` names a function. An implicit-`it` body is not a callable, so the same
# synthesis produced `(it * 2)(__hofp0)`: a call whose callee is a BinaryOp.
# The self-host lowerer's `ECall` callee dispatcher then discarded that
# nonsense silently, and the accumulator was filled with unmapped,
# uninitialized elements — a SIGSEGV as soon as the result was read back.
#
# The implicit closure's single parameter is spelled `it`, exactly as
# `compute_implicit_closure_sig` types it, so the body is extracted with that
# binding instead of being wrapped.
#
# TWO AXES, because two different things had to be true.
#
# AXIS 1 — the HOF ARM. Every collection higher-order method that takes a
# single-parameter body, so a partial regression at one arm trips this fixture:
#   map (cross-type accumulator) · filter (predicate) · any · all ·
#   Set.any (a different iteration producer)
# `Option.map(it * 2)` is NOT here: Option combinators lower through their own
# arm and never reach `extract_hof_closure` (see `implicit_it.gg`).
#
# AXIS 2 — the BODY SHAPE, i.e. how the body reaches `it`. Whether an argument
# IS an implicit-`it` body is decided by one predicate, `expr_has_it`, and that
# predicate named 8 of `Expr`'s 44 variants. A body it answered `false` for was
# treated as a CALLABLE and wrapped `(body)(__hofp0)` — so the arithmetic form
# `it * 2` worked while every body that reaches `it` through a PROJECTION did
# not, in a different way each time:
#   `it * 2`       arithmetic          — the shape the eight arms covered
#   `it.n`         FIELD projection    — SEGV (the wrap became `(it.n)(__hofp0)`)
#   `it[0]`        INDEX projection    — SEGV, a different walker arm again
#   `it.n > 1`     projection under a comparison — SILENTLY EMPTY vector
#   `if it > 1: 7 else: 0`   `it` in the CONDITION, constant branches
#   `if flag: it else: 0`    `it` only in a BRANCH TAIL, none in the condition
#   `if it > 1: it else: 0`  both
# The three `if` cells fail for a SECOND reason, and it is worth stating
# separately because it is not this predicate at all: the DECLARED type of the
# destination used to leak into the body. `Vector[int] ys = xs.map(if …)` typed
# the if-expression's tail slot from that `Vector[int]`, `comp_note_elem`
# carried it as the ELEMENT type, and `ys` was minted `Vector[Vector[int]]`
# and read back as pointer garbage — while `auto ys = …` was correct all along.
# The comprehension producer now clears the outer expectation for the duration
# of the body: the element type is what it exists to LEARN.
#
# NAMED OMISSIONS on axis 2 (Core #12) — each CHECKED against Rust `gg` before
# being left out, because an omission is only honest if the reference agrees it
# is not a cell:
#   `it as float`                  Rust REJECTS (E_TypeMismatch on `?T0`)
#   inline `match` / `do:` bodies  Rust REJECTS (parse errors)
#   comprehension-bodied           Rust ICEs
#   a MULTI-STATEMENT block body   needs a `Stmt` walk `expr_has_it` does not
#                                  have; no reference program spells one here
# The second axis is why the predicate is now an exhaustive walk that stops
# only at closure boundaries, rather than a working set.
#
# The failure was INVISIBLE to a fixture that only reads the result's LENGTH.
# `closure_mixed_implicit_explicit_wiring.gg` was green throughout because
# `map`'s accumulator had the right element COUNT and only the values were
# garbage — SIX QUESTIONS #6, a green cell green for a reason unrelated to
# what it tests. That fixture now reads its values too; this one is the wide
# net.
#
# ⚠ THIS FIXTURE LEAKS, AND THE LEAK IS NOT ITS OWN. Rust `gg` leaks one
# 8-byte closure environment per implicit-`it` HOF call — a pre-existing class
# the allowlist header has characterised since the first corpus sweep ("every
# builtin HOF call (map/filter/fold) leaks an 8-byte env, UNBOUNDED per
# call") — and MEASURED WIDER than that by `todo/t0953`, which owns it: a plain
# user function taking a `Callable` leaks the same frame with no builtin HOF
# anywhere in the program, so the write site is the closure-literal ARGUMENT,
# not the HOF expander. Eleven calls here, eleven records; the row in
# `tests/sanitize/LEAK_ALLOWLIST.txt` cites that item. Admitting the row is
# deliberate: a leak that is declared and counted by the trend gate gets
# fixed, and one hidden outside the swept corpus does not. Do NOT trim the
# axis to shrink the row — the arms are the coverage.
#
# INTENDED (asserted by the wired test): the projected elements, then the
# mapped and filtered ones, then three `true`s. Once `todo/t0953` is fixed,
# this fixture also runs ASan-clean and its allowlist row is deleted.

struct Row:
    int n

void main():
    Vector[int] xs = [1, 2, 3, 4]

    # AXIS 2 — a body that reaches `it` through a FIELD projection, and one
    # that reaches it through a projection under a comparison. Neither was
    # recognised as an implicit-`it` body at all.
    Vector[Row] rows = [Row(1), Row(2), Row(3)]
    Vector[int] ns = rows.map(it.n)
    for n in ns:
        print(f"proj {n}")
    Vector[Row] big = rows.filter(it.n > 1)
    for b in big:
        print(f"projfilt {b.n}")
    Vector[Vector[int]] grid = [[10, 11], [20, 21]]
    Vector[int] heads = grid.map(it[0])
    for h in heads:
        print(f"idx {h}")
    # `it` in an `if` — in the condition, in a branch tail, and in both. The
    # declared `Vector[int]` on the left is load-bearing: with `auto` these
    # were green throughout.
    Vector[int] pick = xs.map(if it > 1: 7 else: 0)
    for pk in pick:
        print(f"cond {pk}")
    bool flag = true
    Vector[int] branch = xs.map(if flag: it else: 0)
    for br in branch:
        print(f"branch {br}")
    Vector[int] both = xs.map(if it > 1: it else: 0)
    for bo in both:
        print(f"both {bo}")

    Vector[int] doubled = xs.map(it * 2)
    for d in doubled:
        print(f"map {d}")

    Vector[int] evens = xs.filter(it % 2 == 0)
    for e in evens:
        print(f"filter {e}")

    print(f"any {xs.any(it > 3)}")
    print(f"all {xs.all(it > 0)}")

    Set[int] s = {1, 2, 3}
    print(f"set_any {s.any(it == 2)}")
